-funded incumbent reflects that evidence without overextending a thin polling sample.", "watch": "A major scandal or a sharp shift in the national environment could narrow the gap, though nothing in the current evidence points that way.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Wes Moore (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Maryland's governor's race is a rematch of 2022, with incumbent Democrat Wes Moore facing Republican Dan Cox. Every major race rating, including Cook Political Report, Inside Elections, and Sabato's Crystal Ball, places the contest in the Solid or Safe Democratic column. The two most recent polls, from UMBC in late September and Zenith in August, show Moore ahead by roughly 27 points among likely voters, and betting markets put his chances near 97 to 98 percent.\n\nMoore has raised more than $15.5 million and holds $6.5 million on hand, while Cox has raised about $242,000. The state's 15-point Democratic lean, Moore's incumbency, and his national profile all point the same direction. A Green Party contender has raised only about $38,000, and coverage notes third-party candidates face long historical odds here. The open question is the size of Moore's victory margin, not which candidate wins.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 19.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9967, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9939, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9968, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9958, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9956, "margin": 19.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9959, "margin": 19.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9942, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9961, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.9947, "margin": 20.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.9966, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.9972, "margin": 23.0}, {"date": "2026-1